- TRAVERSAL OWNERSHIP: This domain owns movement, travel, mounts, terrain, and environmental hazard
  checks. It does NOT own weapon attacks, spell attacks, damage rolls, or combat save prompts.
  If the action is "move into combat", "charge toward them", or otherwise just repositions for a
  fight, return EMPTY player_rolls unless the movement itself triggers a traversal check.
- ROUTINE TRAVEL: walking, riding a trained mount on a road, or other routine travel actions
  require NO roll unless terrain, hazards, navigation pressure, or mount-handling complications
  introduce an actual traversal mechanic.
- TRAVEL: Do NOT estimate travel time or distance — the TimeKeeper step handles that. Focus on
  whether the travel action requires any mechanical checks (Survival for navigation, Fortitude
  for forced march, etc.).
- ENVIRONMENTAL HAZARDS: account for swim checks, climb checks, jump checks, and weather effects
  (visibility penalties, movement reduction in heavy rain/snow).
- SWIM: calm water DC 10, rough water DC 15, stormy water DC 20. Must make a check each round
  or sink. Failure by 5+ means going under (drowning risk). STR or CON check to avoid fatigue
  after extended swimming.
- CLIMB: knotted rope DC 5, rough stone wall DC 15, smooth stone wall DC 25, overhang or ceiling
  DC 20. Failure by 5+ means falling from current height. Move at half speed while climbing.
  Cannot use a shield while climbing without a DC 25 check.
- JUMP (Acrobatics): running long jump DC = distance in feet (requires 10ft running start).
  Standing long jump DC = double the distance. High jump DC = 4x height in feet. Failure means
  falling short — Reflex save to catch a ledge if one is available.
- MOVEMENT IN DIFFICULT TERRAIN: each square costs 2 squares of movement. Running and charging
  are not possible in difficult terrain unless the character has special abilities.
- MOUNTED MOVEMENT: a trained mount moves at its own speed. Ride check DC 5 to guide with knees
  (free hands for combat). DC 15 to fight with mount moving. Difficult terrain affects mount speed.
